#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Mock agent-pane binary for eval scenario

set -e

if [[ "$1" == "page" ]]; then
  shift
  case "$1" in
    --help)
      echo "Usage: agent-pane page <format> [--help]"
      echo ""
      echo "Formats: info, markdown, text, dom, selection"
      exit 0
      ;;
    info)
      echo "URL: https://www.wired.com/story/how-open-source-software-is-eating-the-world"
      echo "Title: How Open Source Software Took Over the World | WIRED"
      exit 0
      ;;
    markdown)
      cat << 'ARTICLE'
# How Open Source Software Took Over the World

**By Steven Levy | October 2026**

URL: https://www.wired.com/story/how-open-source-software-is-eating-the-world

---

Two decades ago, the idea that the world's most critical infrastructure would run on
software built by volunteers sharing code freely seemed laughable. Today, it is simply
the state of things.

Open source software powers the internet's backbone. Linux runs more than 90 percent
of cloud servers worldwide. The Android operating system, based on the Linux kernel,
runs billions of smartphones. Kubernetes orchestrates containers in data centers from
Singapore to São Paulo. Curl, a command-line tool written largely by one developer,
handles data transfer in everything from smart fridges to Tesla dashboards.

The question is no longer whether open source won. It's what victory means for the
software industry and for society.

## The Corporate Embrace

The model of adoption has shifted over the past decade. Major technology companies —
Google, Microsoft, Amazon, Meta — that once viewed open source with suspicion or
hostility now contribute billions of dollars and millions of engineering hours to
open source projects.

"It's not charity," says Nadia Asparouhova, a researcher who has studied open source
sustainability. "These companies depend on the software. Contributing is cheaper than
forking and maintaining your own version."

## The Sustainability Question

Despite corporate investment in flagship projects, thousands of smaller libraries
remain maintained by individuals who do it in their spare time. These libraries often
sit deep in the dependency chains of products used by millions.

The Log4Shell vulnerability of 2021 illustrated the risk starkly. A flaw in a small
Java logging library maintained by volunteers became one of the most exploited
vulnerabilities in history, affecting systems across government and industry.

## What Comes Next

Lawmakers are increasingly focused on software supply chain security. The United States
and European Union have both introduced legislation requiring software makers to
document their dependencies — a kind of nutrition label for code.

Meanwhile, artificial intelligence tools are changing how developers write open source
software. AI coding assistants can now generate boilerplate, suggest fixes, and even
catch common security bugs before they ship.

Whether this accelerates the dominance of open source or complicates the already
thorny questions of attribution, licensing, and sustainability remains an open question.

One thing seems certain: the code that runs the world will, for the foreseeable future,
be code that anyone can read.
ARTICLE
      exit 0
      ;;
    text)
      echo "How Open Source Software Took Over the World"
      echo "By Steven Levy | October 2026"
      echo ""
      echo "Two decades ago, the idea that the world's most critical infrastructure"
      echo "would run on software built by volunteers sharing code freely seemed laughable."
      echo "Today, it is simply the state of things."
      exit 0
      ;;
    dom)
      echo "<html><body><article><h1>How Open Source Software Took Over the World</h1><p>By Steven Levy</p></article></body></html>"
      exit 0
      ;;
    selection)
      echo ""
      exit 0
      ;;
    *)
      echo "agent-pane page: unknown format '$1'" >&2
      echo "Run 'agent-pane page --help' for usage." >&2
      exit 1
      ;;
  esac
else
  echo "Usage: agent-pane <command> [args]"
  echo ""
  echo "Commands:"
  echo "  page    Read the current Chrome tab"
  exit 0
fi
